WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.000
How does a pushdown automaton

2
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.080
guess the middle of a

3
00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.260
palindrome? It pushes a prefix,

4
00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:08.100
nondeterministically chooses where to switch

5
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.060
phases, and compares the suffix

6
00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:11.620
with that prefix in reverse.

7
00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:13.940
A correct midpoint guess can

8
00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:15.600
accept even when other guesses

9
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:16.280
get stuck.

10
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.100
For the even palindrome zero

11
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.320
one one zero, first push

12
00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:22.640
a bottom marker and then

13
00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.040
the initial zero and one.

14
00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:25.760
The stack top is now

15
00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:27.900
one, followed by zero and

16
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.100
the marker. This reversed order

17
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:31.680
is precisely what the remaining

18
00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.080
one zero should match.

19
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.860
Guess the even-length midpoint without

20
00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:37.540
consuming input or changing the

21
00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:39.780
stack. The epsilon read, epsilon

22
00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.920
pop, epsilon push transition only

23
00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.280
changes the finite-control phase. It

24
00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.400
is a guess rather than

25
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:46.840
an empty-stack test, and a

26
00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:48.060
wrong early or late guess

27
00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:49.220
will fail later matching.

28
00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:51.220
Read the next one and

29
00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:52.920
pop the matching one, then

30
00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.540
read zero and pop zero.

31
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.140
The marker is exposed exactly

32
00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:58.780
as the input ends, so

33
00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:00.240
the branch can finish accepting.

34
00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:02.060
A different suffix such as

35
00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:03.500
zero one would mismatch the

36
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:04.640
top and kill this branch.

37
00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.100
For the odd palindrome zero

38
00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:08.840
one zero, push the first

39
00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:10.300
zero but consume the middle

40
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.560
one while switching phases. Do

41
00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:13.960
not push that middle symbol,

42
00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:15.360
because it has no partner

43
00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:16.820
on the other side. The

44
00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:18.420
final zero then matches and

45
00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:19.960
pops the single stored zero.

46
00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:23.300
To recognize all binary palindromes,

47
00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:25.480
provide both the even epsilon

48
00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:27.260
switch and odd switches reading

49
00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.620
zero or one. Epsilon is

50
00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:31.080
handled by guessing an empty

51
00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:32.820
first half and an even

52
00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.420
switch. If the language asks

53
00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:36.520
only for even palindromes, the

54
00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:38.600
odd-switch transitions must be omitted.

55
00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:40.740
Now change the language to

56
00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.680
odd-length words with middle symbol

57
00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:44.500
zero. We still use the

58
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:45.800
stack to compare half lengths,

59
00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:47.540
but no longer compare symbol

60
00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:49.920
values. Push one counting marker

61
00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.100
per initial symbol, switch only

62
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.660
by reading zero, then pop

63
00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:55.480
one marker per remaining symbol

64
00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:56.280
of either kind.

65
00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.400
For example, zero one zero

66
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.140
one one has length five

67
00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.420
and central zero, despite not

68
00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.660
being a palindrome. A counter-stack

69
00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:07.020
branch can push for its

70
00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:08.820
first two symbols, consume the

71
00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:10.180
central zero and pop for

72
00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:12.400
the last two. A palindrome

73
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.300
stack would instead reject when

74
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:15.700
the right symbols fail to

75
00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:16.480
mirror the left.

76
00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:19.900
Pushdown nondeterminism is not automatically

77
00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.340
removable in the way finite-automaton

78
00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:26.280
nondeterminism is. Deterministic pushdown automata

79
00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.200
recognize a strictly smaller class

80
00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.060
than nondeterministic ones. The unmarked

81
00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:32.640
midpoint is an important reason

82
00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.640
the general palindrome construction uses

83
00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:35.060
a guess.

84
00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:36.840
Things to take note of.

85
00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:38.940
A stack reverses the stored

86
00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:41.080
prefix, even and odd midpoint

87
00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:43.100
transitions consume different amounts of

88
00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:45.360
input, and every accepted branch

89
00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.540
must finish the word. Distinguish

90
00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.720
palindrome symbol matching from middle-position

91
00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:51.320
length matching, and keep the

92
00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:53.040
requested parity and base cases

93
00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:53.480
explicit.
